    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2028 Mitsubishi Outlander Diamond Trail Edition: How Does It Compare to Other Adventure Crossovers?\nNew Collaboration to Deploy Eve’s Embraer-Backed eVTOL to Southern Brazil\n8 photos\nPhoto: Eve Air Mobility\nUnlike some other eVTOL concepts that made headlines right away only to fizzle down shortly after, due to financial difficulties, the Brazilian Eve Air Mobility took things slowly and grew steadily, becoming one of the most secure and sought-after names in the emerging AAM industry worldwide.\nThis constant growth has a lot to do with the fact that Eve Air Mobility didn’t begin as an ambitious but inexperienced startup. Instead, it was established as the AAM (Advanced Air Mobility) subsidiary of Embraer, the Brazilian aircraft maker with a solid global reputation and a strong market presence.\nThat is why, although not necessarily one of the most innovative or striking eVTOL designs out there, Eve has already earned a top position among international competitors. The aircraft concept itself was further backed by a comprehensive, dedicated system of services and operational support, and this integrative approach made Eve even more appealing for operators.\nOne of the most recent purchase agreement will focus specifically on Southern Brazil, where the Eve eVTOL could really herald a new form of urban connectivity. This is what Gustavo Zanettini, co-founder and Chief Executive Officer of FlyBIS, believes. FlyBIS is one of the few young operators bold enough to bet exclusively on AAM as a sustainable and more efficient alternative to conventional air mobility.\nFlyBIS and Eve first shook hands in 2022, and now it was time to take things to the next level through a firm order for two Eve aircraft and purchase rights for ten more units. This could mean that up to 12 eVTOLs will start operating in key areas across Southern Brazil in the near future.\nSouthern Brazil has two things that make it ripe for air taxi activity, namely strong tourism and intense business activity. Things aren’t set in stone, but FlyBIS predicts several air taxi routes along Santa Catarina’s coastline, with connections in cities like Navegantes and Florianopolis. Other routes could focus on tourism destinations in the mountain region of Rio Grande do Sul.\nAs usual with these types of agreements in an emerging sector of air mobility, the players need to handle complicated issues behind the scenes, from planning future networks and making sure that the right infrastructure is in place, to working with authorities for the safe integration of air taxi services into the wider transportation system.\nSo far, the full-scale prototype of the Eve eVTOL has completed 70 flights as part of an ongoing flight campaign. Transition flights will continue, as the Brazilian eVTOL reaches for the final stage of wing-borne flight. Thanks to Eve, Brazil has the potential to become one of the first countries to secure commercial-scale production for their domestic aircraft right after certification.\nThe Eve eVTOL has a four-passenger (plus pilot) configuration and it promises a fully electric-powered range of up to 100 km (60 miles).\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBrazilian eVTOL Eve Air Mobility embraer Eve eVTOL evtol air taxi\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT They Bought a Lexus LC for $123,494, Put It Up for Sale Immediately, Refused $163,500",
